Body:
What if you allow an option to add aliased versions of a provider? E.g. in your opencode.json

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "enabled_providers": ["github-copilot", "github-copilot-work"],
  "provider_alias": {
    "github-copilot-work": "github-copilot"
  } 
}
```

Then in auth.json, you can use the alias for the aliased provider and fall back to the regular provider name if there is no alias? So basically, an aliased provider would act like a locally defined completely separate provider that is referenced everywhere by its alias, and in the config, we describe what type it is?

I originally opened this as a feature request, but after tracing the implementation this looks more like a bug in the existing model override behavior.
Specifically, `provider.models.<model>.limit.input` is currently ignored in two places:
1. config validation does not allow partial `limit` overrides such as:
   ```json
   {
     "provider": {
       "anthropic": {
         "models": {
           "claude-sonnet-4-20250514": {
             "limit": { "input": 123456 }
           }
         }
       }
     }
   }
2. during provider model merging, limit.input is not preserved/applied at runtime.
This means model-level input limit overrides are ineffective even though model overrides already exist for other fields.
I'm preparing a small fix that:
- allows partial limit overrides in config
- preserves and applies limit.input at runtime
- adds regression tests

